livekit-libwebrtc — maintainer notes
====================================

What this package is
--------------------
WebRTC milestone 137 from the webrtc-sdk fork, built the way the LiveKit Rust
SDK builds it, but as a shared library instead of the static archive LiveKit
ships, and against the distribution's media libraries wherever that works.

  source:  https://github.com/webrtc-sdk/webrtc
  commit:  0001d84a16a3502e45b86a065253df4c511026ae  (branch m137_release,
           2025-10-12, "Remove extension check macro (#202)")
  recipe:  zed-industries/livekit-rust-sdks, rev 0a1c519cfce9b365229026b55de9b9dbdb6fed3c,
           file webrtc-sys/libwebrtc/build_linux.sh, release tag webrtc-0001d84-4
  shared-library form after nixpkgs pkgs/by-name/li/livekit-libwebrtc

The trailing "-4" in the LiveKit tag is a rebuild counter for the same webrtc
commit, which is why it is not part of Version:.

Consumer contract
-----------------
The webrtc-sys crate downloads a prebuilt archive from GitHub unless

    LK_CUSTOM_WEBRTC=%{_libdir}/livekit-webrtc

is set.  OBS has no network, so every consumer must set it.  webrtc-sys expects
these entries below that directory:

    lib/libwebrtc.so          symlink to %{_libdir}/libwebrtc-suse.so.137
    include/                  the header tree; it adds include/,
                              include/third_party/abseil-cpp and
                              include/third_party/libyuv/include to its
                              include path
    webrtc.ninja              first line parsed for -D... defines
    desktop_capture.ninja     likewise; this is the file that carries
                              -DWEBRTC_USE_X11, webrtc.ninja does not
    args.gn                   copied for reference, not read by the build

webrtc-sys hard-codes a static link.  The consumer's %prep has to change that
one line, as nixpkgs does for Zed:

    sed -i 's/cargo:rustc-link-lib=static=webrtc/cargo:rustc-link-lib=dylib=webrtc/' \
        webrtc-sys/build.rs

`dylib=webrtc` plus the crate's own link-search path resolves through
lib/libwebrtc.so, and DT_NEEDED then records the real soname; nothing generic
called libwebrtc.so* is installed in %{_libdir}.

Everything else webrtc uses (X11, Xfixes, Xdamage, Xrandr, Xcomposite, Xext,
drm, gbm, alsa, pulse, pipewire, and libva on x86_64) is reached through dlopen
stubs; webrtc-sys itself pkg-config-probes glib-2.0, gobject-2.0 and gio-2.0.

soname and the ABI guard
------------------------
Upstream publishes no shared library and therefore no soname, so this package
invents one and puts it in the SUSE namespace: `libwebrtc-suse.so.137`, shipped
by `libwebrtc-suse137`.  The number follows the WebRTC milestone and moves when
the milestone does.

A milestone is far too coarse for the real ABI: WebRTC's C++ interfaces change
between snapshots of the same branch.  The linker version script therefore puts
every symbol in a node named after the snapshot,

    LIVEKIT_WEBRTC_137_0001d84

so rpm generates
`libwebrtc-suse.so.137(LIVEKIT_WEBRTC_137_0001d84)(64bit)` on both sides and
ld.so refuses to load a consumer built against a different snapshot instead of
crashing later.  Nothing else is needed in the consumer's spec: the dependency
comes out of DT_NEEDED plus the version reference automatically, and it is a
tighter guard than a hand-written `= %{version}` would be.  Consumers do have
to be rebuilt on every bump of this package.

Lockstep rule — read this before every Zed bump
-----------------------------------------------
Zed pins zed-industries/livekit-rust-sdks in its Cargo.lock.  At that revision,
webrtc-sys/build/src/lib.rs has

    pub const WEBRTC_TAG: &str = "webrtc-<short commit>-<rebuild>";

Re-read it on every Zed bump.  A changed tag means the artifact Zed links
against changed; resolve the short commit against
https://github.com/webrtc-sdk/webrtc and, if the commit moved, bump this package
FIRST, then Zed.  A tag whose rebuild counter alone moved (-3 -> -4) is the same
webrtc commit and needs no source bump here, but the patch set in
webrtc-sys/libwebrtc/patches must still be diffed against ours.  A new commit
also means a new `%global commit_short`, hence a new version node, hence a
mandatory rebuild of every consumer.

Do not take the recipe from upstream livekit/rust-sdks: its master pins a
different webrtc commit.  The Zed fork is the authority.

Regenerating the source tarball
-------------------------------
There is no upstream tarball for a gclient checkout, so Source0 is generated by
make-source.sh (Source1):

    bash make-source.sh

Everything that feeds the archive is pinned or fixed - the webrtc commit, the
depot_tools commit, gclient's own revision list, and a tar with --sort=name,
the commit's committer timestamp, numeric owner 0:0 and a fixed mode - so two
runs of the same script should produce the same bytes.  That has not been
verified by running it twice; the hash below is from the run that produced the
shipped file.

It needs network and about 11 GB of scratch space, and takes ~6 minutes.  It

  * pins depot_tools to 4a978d8f1f3567d5bd729aec018bfc345a14e1cd,
  * runs gclient sync --no-history --nohooks --shallow at the webrtc commit,
  * prunes what the :default gn graph never reads — .git, the prebuilt
    toolchains that arrive as DEPS entries rather than hooks (clang, rust,
    jdk, siso, ninja, gn), Chromium trees outside webrtc (blink, catapult
    payload, grpc, tflite_support), test payloads, other platforms, and the
    sources of every library that compiles into nothing,
  * empties every unbundled library's tree: ffmpeg and openh264 down to a
    placeholder BUILD.gn (their licences must not ship), dav1d, libaom,
    libjpeg_turbo, libvpx, opus and zlib down to their build files and
    licences, which is what replace_gn_files.py overwrites,
  * drops the trees whose licence would otherwise need reviewing even though
    nothing reads their build files - widevine (not open source), unrar
    (non-free), mutter (GPL-2.0) - and the crash handlers and test corpora
    beside them,
  * deletes every already-compiled file it can find: the explicitly known
    payload directories plus a `file`-magic sweep for ELF, PE, Mach-O, ar,
    wasm and Java archives anywhere in the tree, logged by name when it runs.
    The sweep runs `file` serially on purpose: under xargs -P several writers
    share one pipe, lines interleave and the filter silently misses entries -
    that is how two binaries survived into an earlier tarball,
  * writes two stubs the gn run insists on:
    third_party/rust-toolchain/VERSION (no Rust is compiled; build/config/
    rust.gni still exec_scripts update_rust.py, which fails without it) and
    build/util/LASTCHANGE.committime, which build/util/version.gni reads
    whenever use_dummy_lastchange=true is not passed — the spec does pass it,
    so the stub is belt and braces,
  * tars with --sort=name, the webrtc commit's committer timestamp and
    --mode=u+rwX,go+rX,go-w, then zstd -19.

To move to a new commit, edit the commit variable at the top of the script and
re-run it; the file name and the mtime follow from the commit.

  tarball:  webrtc-m137-0001d84.tar.zst, 107.9 MiB
  sha256:   61b4ead190400a78b949b982fef914df1ea3b2a48e4acb9aba72ff99b420b896
  zstd:     1.5.7

gn arguments
------------
livekit's webrtc-sys/libwebrtc/build_linux.sh list, plus what it takes to build
against the distribution and to produce one shared library.

  is_debug=false                 release build
  target_os/target_cpu           native only, set from %ifarch
  rtc_enable_protobuf=false      livekit does not use the protobuf RTC log
  treat_warnings_as_errors=false a new toolchain must not break the build
  use_custom_libcxx=false        link the distribution's libstdc++, so the C++
  use_custom_libcxx_for_host=    ABI matches the shim cargo compiles
  use_llvm_libatomic=false       no LLVM runtime available
  use_libcxx_modules=false       no C++ modules with GCC
  rtc_include_tests=false        tests, tools and examples are neither built
  rtc_build_tools=false          nor present in the tarball
  rtc_build_examples=false
  rtc_libvpx_build_vp9=true      VP9 and AV1 encoding wanted
  enable_libaom=true
  is_component_build=true        turns rtc_library into source_set, which is
                                 what pulls all of WebRTC into the library
  rtc_enable_symbol_export=true  export the WebRTC API
  rtc_use_h264=true              H.264 through the system openh264/ffmpeg
  rtc_system_openh264=true
  rtc_use_h265=false             ffmpeg-8 ships no hevc decoder; needs
                                 h265-optional-in-frame-cryptor.patch
  rtc_use_pipewire=true          Wayland screen capture
  rtc_use_x11=true               X11 screen capture
  symbol_level=1                 line tables for the debuginfo package
  enable_iterator_debugging=     release STL
  use_rtti=true                  webrtc-sys' shim needs RTTI
  use_sysroot=false              build against the distribution's headers
  is_clang=false                 build with GCC, needs force_gcc.patch
  use_dummy_lastchange=true      no git checkout to stamp a revision from

ffmpeg_branding and is_component_ffmpeg must NOT be passed: both are declared
only in third_party/ffmpeg/ffmpeg_options.gni, which nothing imports once the
unbundle shim has replaced third_party/ffmpeg/BUILD.gn, and gn rejects an
argument no build file reads.  The same goes for any argument belonging to a
library that gets unbundled.

System libraries
----------------
%prep runs

    build/linux/unbundle/replace_gn_files.py --system-libraries \
        dav1d ffmpeg libaom libjpeg libvpx openh264 opus zlib

which swaps each bundled BUILD.gn for a shim that pkg-configs the system copy
and generates forwarding headers.  `rtc_system_openh264=true` is what makes
modules/video_coding use the system openh264.

Two of those need a little more:

  * zlib's shim also declares Chromium's minizip and zip wrappers, and zip
    depends on //base, which a WebRTC checkout does not contain — gn then fails
    on //base/test's reference to the pruned test_fonts.  %prep cuts the file
    off at the minizip shim; nothing here uses either target.
  * openh264's shim generates its headers under
    gen/shim_headers/openh264_shim/third_party/openh264/src/codec/api/wels/,
    which is exactly the path h264_encoder_impl.h includes — but WebRTC's
    rtc_system_openh264 branch never depends on the shim, so the headers are
    not generated.  system-openh264-shim.patch adds that dependency.

The ffmpeg shim only carries headers, so shared-library.patch also names
-lavcodec -lavformat -lavutil in ldflags, as nixpkgs does.  --as-needed then
drops libavformat: WebRTC parses bare codec packets and never demuxes.

Kept bundled, and why
---------------------
  abseil-cpp   the absl_*.gn shims declare only the targets Chromium uses.
               WebRTC wants finer ones and gn stops with "Unresolved
               dependencies": absl/algorithm:algorithm,
               absl/base:dynamic_annotations, absl/base:fast_type_id,
               absl/base:log_severity, absl/base:raw_logging_internal,
               absl/container:layout, absl/log:absl_vlog_is_on,
               absl/log:log_entry, absl/log:log_sink,
               absl/log:log_sink_registry.
  libyuv       the tree compiles against libyuv internals: its own row.h
               includes libyuv/cpu_support.h, which the system libyuv does not
               install (fatal error in api/video/yuv_helper).  webrtc-sys also
               puts include/third_party/libyuv/include on its include path, so
               the headers have to ship either way.
  boringssl    deliberate, and symbol-prefixed; see below.
  libsrtp      WebRTC's own fork.
  rnnoise      modified in-tree.
  crc32c, perfetto, pffft, sigslot   no unbundle shim exists.

Each of those carries a Provides: bundled().

H.264 and H.265
---------------
H.264 goes through the system openh264 plus ffmpeg.  Note that openSUSE's
openh264 may be the `noopenh264` stub on installations that have not enabled
the Cisco repository: the library still links and loads, but encoding and
decoding fail at run time and a call falls back to VP8/VP9/AV1.  That is a
deployment property, not a build one.

H.265 is off (rtc_use_h265=false).  The distribution's ffmpeg-8 is configured
with --disable-decoders and an explicit --disable-decoder=...,hevc,..., so the
hevc decoder is not compiled in; only the HEVC parameter-set parser survives,
for the hevc_metadata and hevc_mp4toannexb bitstream filters.  (Checked on the
shipped libavcodec.so.62: hevcdec.c's own strings such as "Could not find ref
with POC" are absent while hevc_ps.c's "VPS %d does not exist" is present.)
Note that rtc_use_h265 never added a codec anyway - it only builds
common_video/h265/* parsers, the RTP (de)packetizers and the SDP name.

Turning it off needs h265-optional-in-frame-cryptor.patch: livekit's
api/crypto/frame_crypto_transformer.cc calls webrtc::H265::FindNaluIndices,
ParseNaluType, WriteRbsp and ParseRbsp with no build guard, so the library does
not link without them.  The patch puts those branches behind
#if defined(RTC_ENABLE_H265); each was already behind a runtime FrameIsH265()
check, so nothing changes when H.265 is on.  api/crypto:frame_crypto_transformer
itself stays in add_deps.patch - it is LiveKit's end-to-end encryption, which
Zed uses.

That patch is upstreamable to webrtc-sdk/webrtc and is owed to them; it has not
been sent.

How the shared library is produced
----------------------------------
shared-library.patch carries the edits nixpkgs makes with substituteInPlace plus
the essential half of its 0001-shared-libraries.patch:

  * BUILD.gn: rtc_static_library("webrtc") -> rtc_shared_library("webrtc"),
    dropping complete_static_lib and the thin_archive suppression.
  * BUILD.gn config("common_inherited_config"): cflags -fvisibility=default,
    and on Linux the ffmpeg libraries, the soname and a --version-script
    pointing at //libwebrtc.version.
  * webrtc.gni: the assert that refuses is_component_build.
  * build/config/BUILDCONFIG.gn: the component() template stays static.
  * rtc_tools/BUILD.gn: frame_analyzer, which has no target under a component
    build and leaves the graph unresolved; nixpkgs drops it the same way.

The last two are ours, not nixpkgs'.  is_component_build=true is needed only
because it turns rtc_library into source_set; left alone it would also split
boringssl, abseil, zlib, protobuf_lite and perfetto into their own .so files.
nixpkgs lives with that and installs libthird_party_boringssl.so beside
libwebrtc.so; we do not, because BoringSSL in its own .so cannot be
symbol-prefixed (see below).

The soname is written as @SONAME@ in the patch and substituted in %prep, so the
spec stays the single source of truth for it.  patchelf --set-soname works too
but rewrites the whole ELF.

libwebrtc.version (Source4) carries the ABI node described above and a local:
list.  -fvisibility=default is needed for the WebRTC API but exports everything
else too, so the script takes back what must not be interposable:

  * pw_* and spa_* (from nixpkgs): PipeWire's lazy-load trampolines would
    shadow the real libpipewire when libasound loads its PipeWire plugin, and
    the consumer crashes.
  * perfetto, by mangled prefix.  It is WebRTC's internal tracing and nothing
    outside calls it.

Abseil is deliberately NOT hidden, against first instinct.  Two findings
settled it:

  * There is no collision to avoid.  Chromium builds abseil with
    ABSL_OPTION_USE_INLINE_NAMESPACE 0, so our symbols are absl::Foo; the
    distribution keeps upstream's LTS namespace, so its are
    absl::lts_20260817::Foo - and even the extern "C" entry points differ
    (AbslInternalMutexYield vs AbslInternalMutexYield_lts_20260817).  Checked
    by nm on Factory's libabsl_strings/base/synchronization.  The two sets are
    disjoint by construction; that is exactly what upstream's LTS namespace is
    for.  %check asserts our library exports no lts_ name.
  * Hiding it breaks the consumer.  webrtc-sys links out-of-line abseil through
    WebRTC's headers - video_decoder_factory.cpp calls
    absl::EqualsIgnoreCase(std::string_view, std::string_view) - so a local:
    absl rule turns into an undefined reference at the consumer's link.

Everything else stays exported: the webrtc-sys shim links against a wide slice
of the internal API.

The shim was compiled and linked against the built library to check this, with
-Wl,--no-undefined and the include flags build.rs passes.  Coverage is partial
and will stay so until Zed is packaged: 5 of 28 .cpp files and 6 of 31 public
headers compile standalone, the rest need rust/cxx.h and the cxx-generated
*.rs.h, which only a cargo build produces.  Redo it with the real generated
headers when the consumer exists.

Chromium's component build also adds RUNPATH $ORIGIN so a shared library can
find its siblings; there is only one library here, so shared-library.patch
turns that config off.

BoringSSL symbol prefixing
--------------------------
objcopy --redefine-syms only rewrites .symtab, never .dynsym: run against a
finished shared library it changes nothing the dynamic linker sees.  (This was
measured; it means the same step in nixpkgs' postBuild is a no-op.)  So %build
runs ninja, renames the symbols in every object file, and runs ninja again to
relink.  Two details make that second pass cheap and correct: each object's
mtime is restored after objcopy, because ninja treats an object newer than its
recorded dependency scan as stale and would recompile it; and the archives and
the library are deleted, which is what makes ninja redo exactly those steps.

livekit's boringssl_prefix_symbols.txt was extracted from BoringSSL as of m114
and covers 2763 of the symbols this M137 tree exports; the rest are added at
build time from nm of the BoringSSL objects, for about 3.8k renames in total
(the exact number is architecture-dependent — the assembly implementations
differ).  Only unmangled names are added: those objects also carry libstdc++
vague-linkage symbols such as _ZZNSt19_Sp_make_shared_tag5_S_tiEvE5__tag, which
must keep one address per process.  %check asserts that nothing
SSL_/EVP_/CRYPTO_/X509_ is exported unprefixed, which is what catches a rename
list that has gone stale.

What -devel ships
-----------------
Not the whole tree.  webrtc-sys compiles its C++ shim against a small part of
it, and the rest carries licences nothing here links (GPL mutter and ffmpeg
filters, Commercial widevine, UnRAR, MS-PL wtl, APSL).  The installed set is

    api audio call common_audio common_video logging media modules net p2p pc
    rtc_base sdk stats system_wrappers video
    third_party/{abseil-cpp,libyuv/include,boringssl/src/include,perfetto/include}

which was validated by preprocessing every webrtc-sys/src/*.cpp against exactly
this tree with the include flags build.rs passes: the only headers that come up
missing are jni.h and sdk/android/* (android.cpp, not compiled on Linux) and
the cxx-generated rust/cxx.h and *.rs.h.  Redo that check whenever the shim or
the milestone changes.

Traps worth remembering
-----------------------
  * disable_crel.patch is required on aarch64: Chromium's -Wa,--crel produces
    binaries that segfault there (crbug.com/376278218).
  * tools_webrtc/libs/generate_licenses.py imports depot_tools (not packaged,
    pruned out of the tarball) only to locate gn.py, a wrapper around the gn
    binary; generate-licenses-system-gn.patch calls gn directly.
  * Chromium's gcc_toolchain("arm64") hard-codes an aarch64-linux-gnu- tool
    prefix, which a native aarch64 distribution does not install;
    native-gcc-toolchain.patch empties it, as nixpkgs does.  The x64 gcc
    toolchain already uses unprefixed tool names.  Consequence: this package
    can only be built natively, never cross.
  * src/.gn asks for vpython3; %prep rewrites it to python3.
  * The three patches that do not apply at the top of src/ (disable_crel,
    force_gcc under src/build, david_disable_gun_source_macro under
    src/third_party) have been re-rolled with those prefixes so the whole set
    applies with %autosetup -p1 from the tarball root.  disable_crel.patch also
    had its context indentation repaired; livekit applies it with git apply
    --ignore-space-change, which rpm's patch(1) call does not do.
  * The gn build never sees %{optflags}, so force_gcc.patch carries the
    hardening flags instead: -fstack-protector-strong -fstack-clash-protection
    -D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=3, plus -fcf-protection on x86_64 (aarch64 already gets
    -mbranch-protection=standard from Chromium's own config).  Chromium
    supplies -O2 -fPIC -funwind-tables itself.
  * Leap 16.0 cannot build this: libaom's SVE path includes
    <arm_neon_sve_bridge.h>, which only GCC 14 and newer ship.  The 16.0
    repository is disabled for the package in the build project; a repository
    disable does not travel with a submit request, so say it in the request
    text if the target project has a Leap 16.0 repository.

Other unbundling candidates
---------------------------
The libraries nixpkgs additionally unbundles (brotli, fontconfig, freetype,
harfbuzz-ng, icu, jsoncpp, libpng, libwebp, libxml, libxslt) contribute no
object to the :default graph at all, so make-source.sh deletes their sources
instead of replacing them.

Debuginfo
---------
symbol_level=1 makes Chromium pass -g1 -gdwarf-4 to GCC
(build/config/compiler/BUILD.gn), which is what the -debuginfo package is built
from where debuginfo is enabled - Factory and editors both enable it.

In a project that does not, rpm's own /usr/lib/rpm/brp-suse.d/brp-15-strip-debug
runs instead of find-debuginfo: the library is stripped of .debug_* but keeps
.symtab, and rpmlint then reports unstripped-binary-or-object.  That is a
property of the build project, not of the spec.
